{{also|Sky|SKY|S.K.Y.|ský|-sky|-ský}}
==Translingual==

===Symbol===
{{mul-symbol}}

# {{ISO 639|3}}

===See also===
* {{langcat}}

==English==
{{was wotd|2021|October|18}}

===Pronunciation===
* {{enPR|skī|a=RP,GA}}, {{IPA|en|/skaɪ/}}
* {{audio|en|En-uk-sky.ogg|a=RP}}
* {{audio|en|En-us-sky.ogg|a=GA}}
* {{homophones|en|Sky|Skye}}
* {{rhymes|en|aɪ|s=1}}

===Etymology 1===
[[File:A blue sky8.JPG|thumb|A blue sky ''(sense 1)''.]]
{{root|en|ine-pro|*(s)kewH-}}
The {{glossary|noun}} is derived from  {{inh|en|enm|sky|t=sky; cloud; mist}}, also spelled {{m|enm|ski}}, {{m|enm|skie}}, {{nb...|schi, schie, schye, sci, scki, scky, scy, skei, skiʒe, skiȝe, sky, skye, (Early Middle English) skige|otherforms=1}},<ref>{{R:MED Online|entry=skī(e|pos=n|id=MED40669}}</ref> from {{der|en|non|ský|t=cloud}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*skiwją|t=cloud; sky}}, from {{m|gem-pro|*skiwô|t=cloud; cloud cover, haze; sky}} (whence {{cog|ang|sċēo|t=cloud}} and {{cog|enm|skew|t=air; sky; (''rare'') cloud}}), from {{der|en|ine-pro|*(s)kewH-|t=to cover; to conceal, hide}}.<ref>Compare {{R:OED Online|pos=''n.''{{sup|1}}|noformat=1|id=181136|date=September 2021|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=n}}</ref>

Partly displaced {{ncog|ang|heofon}}, which survives in the reflex {{m|en|heaven}}, still sometimes used in the sense of ''sky'', but usually in high or poetic register.

The {{glossary|verb}} is derived from the noun.<ref>{{R:OED Online|pos=v|id=181139|date=December 2020|nodot=1}}; {{R:Lexico|pos=v}}</ref>

{{col-top|2|cog}}
The English word is cognate with {{cog|ang|scēo|t=cloud}}, {{cog|osx|scio}}, {{m|osx|skio}}, {{m|osx|skeo|t=light cloud cover}}, {{cog|da|-}}, {{cog|sv|-}} and {{cog|nb|sky|t=cloud}}, {{cog|sga|ceo|t=mist, fog}}, {{cog|ga|ceo|t=mist, fog}}. It is also related to {{cog|ang|scūa|t=shadow, darkness}}, {{cog|la|obscūrus|t=dark, shadowy}}, {{cog|sa|स्कुनाति|t=he covers}}. See also {{m|en|hide}}, {{m|en|hose}}, {{m|en|house}}, {{m|en|hut}}, {{m|en|shoe}}.
{{col-bottom}}

====Noun====
{{en-noun}}

# {{senseid|en|Q527}}The [[atmosphere]] [[above#Preposition|above]] a [[given#Adjective|given]] [[point#Noun|point]], especially as [[visible]] from the [[surface#Noun|surface]] of the [[Earth]] as the [[place#Noun|place]] where the [[sun#Noun|sun]], [[moon#Noun|moon]], [[star#Noun|stars]], and [[cloud#Noun|clouds]] are [[see#Verb|seen]].
#: {{synonyms|en|blue|firmament|heaven|q3=literary, often plural|q4=chiefly Scotland|lift|q5=literary or poetic, archaic|welkin}}
#: {{ux|en|That year, a meteor fell from the '''sky'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer Q1|act=IV|scene=i|page=49|passage=For beſides the groues, / The '''skyes''', the fountaines, euery region neare / Seeme all one mutuall cry. I neuer heard / So muſicall a diſcord, ſuch ſweete thunder.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|year=1596.2|book=IV|canto=III|stanza=13|page=40|passage=His wearie ghoſt aſſoyld from fleſhly band, / Did not as others wont, directly fly / Vnto her reſt in [[w:Pluto (mythology)|Plutoes]] grieſly land, / Ne into ayre did vaniſh preſently, / Ne chaunged was into a ſtarre in '''sky''': {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Henry 4-2 Q1|act=IV|scene=ii|page=66|passage=[I]f you doe not all ſhew like guilt twoo pences to mee, and I in the cleere '''skie''' of Fame, ore-ſhine you as much as the full moone doth the cindars of the element, (which ſhew like pinnes heads to her) beleeue not the worde of the noble: {{...}}|translation=[I]f you do not all appear like gilt twopences [''i.e.'', counterfeit coins] next to me, and I, in the clear '''sky''' of fame, outshine you as much as the full moon outshines the cinders of the element [''i.e.'', the stars] (which look like pinheads next to the moon), then don't believe me: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Cymbeline|act=V|scene=vi|page=396|column=1|passage=[A] Nobler Sir, ne're liu'd / 'Twixt '''sky''' and ground.}}
#* {{RQ:Evelyn Diary|volume=I|entrydate=1 November 1660|page=327|passage=I went with some of my relations to Court, to shew them his Ma{{sup|ties}} cabinet and closset of rarities; {{...}} Here I saw {{...}} amongst the clocks, one that shew'd the rising and setting of the Sun in y{{sup|e}} Zodiaq, the Sunn represented by a face and raies of gold, upon an azure '''skie''', observing y{{sup|e}} diurnal and annual motion, rising and setting behind a landscape of hills, the work of our famous Fromantel; and severall other rarities.}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Georgics|book=3|page=103|lines=245–248|passage=[T]he cunning Leach ordains / In Summer's Sultry Heats (for then it reigns) / To feed the Females, e're the Sun ariſe, / Or late at Night, when Stars adorn the '''Skies'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Prior Poems|poem=Carmen Seculare|page=164|passage=Through the large Convex of the Azure '''Sky''', / (For thither Nature caſts our common Eye) / Fierce Meteors ſhoot their arbitrary Light, / And Comets march with lawleſs Horror bright; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope et al Odyssey|book=III|page=120|lines=411–412|passage=A length of Ocean and unbounded '''sky''', / Which ſcarce the Sea-fowl in a year o'erfly {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Wordsworth Poems|volume=I|poem=To a Sky-lark|page=81|passage=There is madness about thee, and joy divine / In that song of thine; / Up with me, up with me, high and high, / To thy banqueting-place in the '''sky'''!}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Christmas Carol|pages=154–155|pageref=155|passage=Running to the window, he opened it, and put out his head. No fog, no mist; clear, bright, jovial, stirring, cold; cold, piping for the blood to dance to; Golden sunlight; Heavenly '''sky'''; sweet fresh air; merry bells. Oh, glorious. Glorious!}}
#* {{RQ:Ferguson Zollenstein|chapter=IV|page=40|passage=So this was my future home, I thought! {{...}} Backed by towering hills, the but faintly discernible purple line of the French boundary off to the southwest, a '''sky''' of palest Gobelin flecked with fat, fleecy little clouds, it in truth looked a dear little city; the city of one's dreams.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|author=w:Irving Berlin|title=[[w:Blue Skies (Irving Berlin song)|Blue Skies]]|year=1926|passage=Blue '''skies''' / Smiling at me / Nothing but blue '''skies''' / Do I see}}
# ''With a descriptive word'': the [[part#Noun|part]] of the sky which can be seen from a [[specific#Adjective|specific]] place or at a specific [[time#Noun|time]]; its [[climate]], [[condition#Noun|condition]], etc.
#: {{ux|en|I lay back under a warm Texas '''sky'''.}}
#: {{ux|en|We’re not sure how long the cloudy '''skies''' will last.}}
#* {{RQ:Cowper Poems|title=Truth|page=80|passage=Yon ancient prude, whoſe wither'd features ſhow / She might be young ſome forty years ago, / {{...}} / With boney and unkerchief'd neck defies / The rude inclemency of wintry '''ſkies''', / And ſails with lappet-head and mincing airs / Duely at clink of bell, to morning pray'rs.}}
#* {{RQ:Wordsworth Coleridge Lyrical Ballads|poem=The Rime of the Ancyent Marinere|part=II|stanza=7|page=13|passage=All in a hot and copper '''sky''' / The bloody sun at noon, / Right up above the mast did stand, / No bigger than the moon.}}
#* {{RQ:Wordsworth Prelude|page=21|passage=[T]he stars / Eastward were sparkling clear, and in the west / The orange '''sky''' of evening died away.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=I|poem=A Dream of Fair Women|stanza=LXVII|page=201|passage=With that sharp sound the white dawn's creeping beams, / Stol'n to my brain, dissolved the mystery / Of folded sleep. The captain of my dreams / Ruled in the eastern '''sky'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Tennyson Maud|stanza=5|pages=58–59|pageref=59|passage=But now shine on, and what care I, / Who in this stormy gulf have found a pearl / The counterclaim of space and hollow '''sky''', {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Vance Outsider|chapter=II|page=378|column=1|passage={{smallcaps|She}} wakened in sharp panic, bewildered by the grotesquerie of some half-remembered dream in contrast with the harshness of inclement fact, drowsily realizing that since she had fallen asleep it had come on to rain smartly out of a shrouded '''sky'''.}}
# {{lb|en|chiefly|literary|and|poetic|archaic}} ''Usually preceded by'' '''[[the]]''': the [[abode]] of [[God]] or the [[god#Noun|gods]], [[angel#Noun|angels]], the [[soul#Noun|souls]] of [[deceased#Adjective|deceased]] [[person|people]], etc.; [[heaven#Noun|heaven]]; also, [[power#Noun|power]]s [[emanate|emanating]] from heaven.
#: {{ux|en|This mortal has incurred the wrath of the '''skies'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Midsummer Q1|act=V|scene=i|page=61|passage=Now am I dead, now am I fled, my ſoule is in the '''sky'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Comus|page=9|passage=Sweet Queen of Parlie, Daughter of the Sphære, / So maist thou be tranſlated to the '''skies''', / And give reſounding grace to all Heav'ns Harmonies.}}
#* {{RQ:Milton Paradise Lost|book=I|lines=44-49|page=10|passage=Him the Almighty Power / Hurld headlong flaming from th' Ethereal '''Skie''' / With hideous ruine and combuſtion down / To bottomleſs perdition, there to dwell / In Adamantine Chains and penal Fire, / Who durſt defie th' Omnipotent to Arms.|year=1873}}
#* {{RQ:Dryden Virgil|chapter=[[w:Eclogues|The Second Pastoral. Or, Alexis.]]|page=8|line=86|passage=The Gods to live in Woods have left the '''Skies'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Prior Poems|poem=Henry and Emma|page=271|passage=''[[w:Mars (mythology)|Mars]]'' ſmil'd and bow'd, the ''Cyprian'' Deity / Turn'd to the glorious Ruler of the '''Sky''': / And Thou, She ſmiling ſaid, Great God of Days / And Verſe; behond my Deed; and ſing my Praiſe.}}
#* {{RQ:Homer Pope Iliad|volume=VI|book=XXII|page=13|lines=218–220|passage=The gazing Gods lean forward from the '''Sky''': / To whom, while eager on the Chace they look, / The Sire of Mortals and Immortals ſpoke.}}
#* {{RQ:Swift Works|volume=VIII|poem=Judas|page=113|passage=By the just vengeance of incensed '''skies''', / Poor bishop {{w|Judas}} late repenting dies.}}
# {{ellipsis of|en|sky blue}}.
#* {{RQ:Dryden Maiden Queen|scene=i|page=26|passage=But yet methinks, thoſe knots of '''Sky''', do not / So well with the dead colour of her Face.}}
#* {{RQ:Etherege She Wou'd|scene=ii|page=39|passage=[W]hy, / Brother, I have beſpoke Dinner, and engag'd / Mr. ''Rake-hell'', the little ſmart Gentleman I have / Often promis'd thee to make thee acquainted / Withal, to bring a whole Bevy of Damſels / In '''Sky''', and Pink, and Flame-colour'd Taffeta's.}}
# {{lb|en|mathematics|[[theoretical physics]]}} The [[set#Noun|set]] of all [[lightlike]] [[line#Noun|lines]] (or [[direction]]s) [[pass#Verb|passing]] through a given point in [[space-time]].
#: {{synonyms|en|celestial sphere}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete|informal|rare}} In an [[art gallery]]: the [[upper#Adjective|upper]] [[row#Noun|rows]] of [[picture#Noun|pictures]] that cannot [[easily]] be seen; also, the place where such pictures are [[hang#Verb|hung]].
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} A cloud. {{defdate|13th–16th c.}}

=====Usage notes=====
In the sense of the firmament, the plural is now mainly literary.

