==English==

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ˈslɪm/|[ˈslɪm]}}
** {{audio|en|en-us-slim.ogg|a=US}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪm|s=1}}
* {{hyph|en|slim}}

===Etymology 1===
Borrowing from {{bor|en|nds|-}} or {{bor|en|nl|slim||bad, sly, clever}}, from {{der|en|dum|slim||bad, crooked}}, from {{der|en|odt|*slimb}}, from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*slimb}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*slimbaz||oblique, crooked}}.  The sense development would have been "slanting, cunning" (Dutch) > "insignificant, slight" and then "thin, graceful" in English, a shift that Liberman calls an "incredible amelioration" of word meaning.<ref>Liberman, A. (2009). Word Origins...And How We Know Them: Etymology for Everyone. United Kingdom: Oxford University Press, USA, p. 200</ref>

The pejorative sense found in Low German and Dutch is also found preserved in the archaic English noun {{m|en|slim|t=worthless or lazy person}}, also comparable to the South African use of the adjective as "crafty, sly."<ref>{{R:New English Dictionary}}</ref>

Compare {{cog|nl|slim||smart, clever, crafty}}, {{cog|gmh|slimp||slanting, awry}}, {{cog|de|schlimm||bad}}, {{cog|fy|slim||bad, dire}}.

