##* {{RQ:Shelley Revolt of Islam|edition=1st|stanza=XXI|page=203|passage=The blasts of autumn drive the winged seeds / Over the earth,—next come the '''snows''', and rain, / And frosts, and storms, which dreary winter leads / Out of his Scythian cave, a savage train; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Kingsley Westward Ho|volume=II|chapter=What Befel at La Guayra|page=301|passage=[G]reat white tassels, swinging from every tree in the breeze which swept down the glade, tossed in their faces a fragrant '''snow''' of blossoms, and glittering drops of perfumed dew.|footer=A figurative use.}}
