[[File:Snowfall in Hockenheim.ogv|thumb|A video showing it snowing ''(verb {{senseno|en|to have snow fall}})'' in [[Hockenheim]], [[Germany]].]]

# {{senseid|en|to have snow fall}}{{lb|en|intransitive|impersonal}} ''Preceded by the [[dummy subject]]'' '''[[it#Pronoun|it]]''': to have [[snow#Noun|snow]] ''(noun {{senseno|en|Q7561}})'' [[fall#Verb|fall]] from the [[atmosphere]].
#: {{ux|en|It is '''snowing'''.&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; It started to '''snow'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Palsgrave Lesclarcissement|book=3rd|chapter=The Table of Verbes|folio=ccclxv|verso=1|column=2|passage=In wynter whan it '''ſnoweth''' it is good ſyttynge by a good fyre: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Dickens Barnaby Rudge|chapter=16|page=21|passage=Then there was the watch with staff and lanthorn crying the hour, and the kind of weather; and those who woke up at his voice and turned them round in bed, were glad to hear it rained, or '''snowed''', or blew, or froze, for very comfort's sake.}}
#* {{quote-song|en|year='''1945''' July (date written)|lyricist=w:Sammy Cahn|composer=w:Jule Styne|artist=w:Vaughn Monroe|title=w:Let It Snow! Let It Snow! Let It Snow!|location=Camden, N.J.|publisher=[[w:RCA Records|RCA Victor]]|year_published=1945|oclc=13337077|passage=Oh! the weather outside is frightful / But the fire is so delightful / And since we've no place to go, / Let it '''snow'''! Let it '''snow'''! Let it '''snow'''!|footer={{small|The score was published as {{cite-book|title=Let It Snow! Let It Snow! Let It Snow!|location=New York, N.Y.|publisher=Edwin H. Morris & Company|year=1945|oclc=1391639812}}.}}}}
## {{lb|en|also|figurative}} Of a [[thing]]: to fall like snow.
##* {{RQ:Young Night-Thoughts|night=5|page=37|passage=''Time'' on this Head has '''ſnovv’d''', yet ſtill ’tis borne / Aloft; nor thinks but on ''another''’s Grave.|footer=A figurative use.}}
##* {{RQ:Heywood Brazen Age|sig=&#91;C4&#93;|verso=1|page=25|passage=She {{quote-gloss|[[w:Diana (mythology)|Diana]]}} hath ſent (to plague vs) a huge ſauadge Boare, / Of an vn-meaſured height and magnitude. / {{...}} / His briſtles poynted like a range of pikes / Ranck't on his backe: his foame '''ſnovves''' vvhere he feeds / His tuskes are like the Indian Oliphants.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Poems 1842|volume=I|poem=The Palace of Art|page=145|passage=And there the Ionian father of the rest; / A million wrinkles carved his skin; / A hundred winters '''snow'd''' upon his breast, / From cheek and throat and chin.}}
##* {{RQ:Baring-Gould Queen of Love|volume=I|chapter=Beggar-my-neighbour|page=153|passage=Then all at once he saw, staring him in the face, a knave of spades. The shock was too great for even his iron nerves; his hand trembled, his fingers involuntarily relaxed, and away shot the cards, flying over the platform and '''snowing''' upon the audience in the front rows.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}}
## To [[cause#Verb|cause]] (something) to fall like snow.
##* {{RQ:Holinshed Chronicles|edition=2nd|volume=III|page=1355|passage=[A]t the ſetting out of a verie ſtatelie tragedie named ''[[w:Dido, Queen of Carthage (play)|Dido]]'', wherein the quéenes banket (with [[w:Aeneas|Eneas]] narration of the deſtruction of Troie) was liuelie deſcribed in a marchpaine patterne, there was alſo a goodlie ſight of hunters with full crie of a kennell of hounds, [[w:Mercury (mythology)|Mercurie]] and [[w:Iris (mythology)|Iris]] deſcending and aſcending from and to an high place, the tempeſt wherein it hailed ſmall confects, rained roſewater, and '''ſnew''' an artificiall kind of ſnow, all ſtrange, maruellous, ⁊ {{quote-gloss|and}} abundant.}}
##* {{RQ:Donne Poems|poem=Falling Star|stanza=2|page=196|passage=Ride ten thouſand daies and nights, / Till age '''ſnovv''' vvhite haires on thee, {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Shakespeare Merry Wives|act=V|scene=v|page=59|column=1|passage=Let the skie raine Potatoes: let it thunder, to the tune of Greene-ſleeues, haile-kiſſing Comfits, and '''ſnovv''' [[eryngo|Eringoes]]: {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Chapman Byron|sig=&#91;Q4&#93;|page=128|passage=[A]s a ''[[savage#Adjective|Sauadge]]'' [[boar|Bore]] that (hunted longe, / Aſſayld and ſet vp) vvith his onely eyes, / Svvimming in fire keepes of the baying hounds, / Though ſuncke himſelfe, yet houlds his anger vp, / And '''ſnovves''' it forth in foame; {{...}} So fares the furious Duke, and vvith his lookes, / Doth teach death horrors; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Scott Canongate|volume=I|pages=xxi–xxii|pageref=xxii|passage=[I]n the situation of the theatrical mechanist, who, when the white paper which represented his shower of snow was exhausted, continued the storm by '''snowing''' brown {{quote-gloss|paper}}, I drew on my memory as long as I could, and, when that failed, eked it out with invention.}}
##* {{RQ:Tennyson Princess|page=15|passage=[H]e started on his feet, / Tore the king's letter, '''snow'd''' it down, and rent / The wonder of the loom thro' warp and woof / From skirt to skirt; {{...}}}}
##* {{RQ:Trowbridge Vagabonds|poem=Service|stanza=9|page=67|passage=If one poor flower of thanks to thee / Be truly given, / All night then '''snowest''' down to me / Lilies of heaven!}}
##* {{RQ:Twain Tom Sawyer|page=140|passage=A sweep of chilly air passed by, rustling all the leaves and '''snowing''' the flaky ashes broadcast about the fire.}}
## To [[cover#Verb|cover]] or [[scatter#Verb|scatter]] (a [[place#Noun|place]] or thing) with, or as if with, snow.
##* {{RQ:Heywood Dramatic Works|volume=IV|play=Londini Sinus Salutis|subchapter=The Speech of [[w:Mars (mythology)|Mars]]|page=295|passage=Even the Horſe wee ride / ''Vnſhod'', would founder, who takes greateſt pride, / When the moſt ''curb'd'', and playing with the ''bit'', / Hee '''ſnowes''' the ground {{quote-gloss|with froth from his mouth}}, and doth the ''Spurre'' forgit.}}
##* {{RQ:Shelley Posthumous Poems|poem=Hymn to Mercury|stanza=XCIV|page=326|passage=There are three {{w|Fates}}, three virgin Sisters, who / Rejoicing in their wind-outspeeding wings, / Their heads with flour '''snowed''' over white and new, / Sit in a vale round which [[Parnassus]] flings / Its circling skirts— {{...}}}}
## {{lb|en|figurative}}
### To cause ([[hair#Noun|hair]]) to [[turn#Verb|turn]] [[white#Adjective|white]]; also, to cause (someone) to have white hair.
###* {{RQ:Sylvester Du Bartas|year=1611|day=2|part=3|page=355|passage=Ah, courteous {{smallcaps|England}}, thy kinde arms I ſee / VVide-stretched out to ſaue and vvelcom me. / Thou (tender Mother) vvilt not ſuffer Age / To '''ſnovve''' my locks in ''Forrein'' Pilgrimage: {{...}}}}
### {{senseid|en|hoodwink}}{{lb|en|originally|US|slang}} To [[convince]] or [[hoodwink#Verb|hoodwink]] (someone), especially by [[present#Verb|presenting]] [[confusing#Adjective|confusing]] [[information]] or through [[flattery]].
###* {{RQ:Bellow Henderson|chapter=XI|page=133|passage={{...}} I concluded that the best thing would be to try to '''snow''' him a little, so I said that I had heard many marvelous reports about the Wariri. As I couldn't think of any details just then, I was just as glad that he didn't ask me to be specific.}}
###* {{quote-song|en|year='''1974''' (date written)|author=w:Donna Weiss; w:Jackie DeShannon|artist=w:Kim Carnes|title=w:Bette Davis Eyes|album=[[w:Mistaken Identity (Kim Carnes album)|Mistaken Identity]]|location=Hollywood, Los Angeles, Calif.|publisher=w:EMI America Records|year_published=10 March 1981|passage=She'll expose you / When she '''snows''' you / 'Cause she knows you}}
#### {{lb|en|poker}} To [[bluff#Verb|bluff]] (an [[opponent]]) in [[draw poker]] by [[play#Verb|playing]] a [[hand#Noun|hand]] which has no [[value#Noun|value]], or by [[refuse#Verb|refusing]] to [[draw#Verb|draw]] any [[card#Noun|cards]].
####* {{quote-book|en|year=1999|month=May|author=w:Mason Malmuth|chapter=Free Bets and Other Topics|title=Gambling Theory and Other Topics|location=Las Vegas, Nev.|publisher=[Creel Printers for] {{w|Two Plus Two Publishing}}|section=part 2 (Theory in Practice)|page=84|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/gamblingtheoryot0000malm/page/284/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-880685-03-7|passage=[T]he Adventurer knew that despite what [[w:Mike Caro|{{quote-gloss|Mike}} Caro]] had said, there was a good chance that he was "'''snowing'''" (playing a hand that had no value and could win only if his opponent threw his cards away). Notice that this creates a dilemma for the Adventurer. If he bets and Caro is on a snow, he will lose a bet, but if he checks and his opponent is not on a snow, he also will lose a bet.}}
### {{lb|en|US|slang|chiefly|passive voice}} To cause (someone) to be under the [[effect#Noun|effect]] of a [[drug#Noun|drug]]; to [[dope#Verb|dope]], to [[drug#Verb|drug]].
###* {{quote-book|en|year=1934|month=July|author=w:Raymond Chandler|chapter=w:Smart-Aleck Kill|title=The Smell of Fear|location=London|publisher=w:Hamish Hamilton|year_published=December 1965|section=section VII|page=202|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/smelloffear0000raym/page/202/mode/1up|oclc=3201812|passage=She looked '''snowed''', weaved around funny, didn't seem to know much what was going on.}}

