===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# The state of being [[singular]], [[distinct]], [[peculiar]], [[uncommon]] or [[unusual]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1618|year_published=1650|author=w:Sir Walter Raleigh|title=The Marrow of Historie, Or, an Epitome of All Historical Passages from the Creation, to the End of the Last Macedonian War|page=40|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=Muc8u-ntE3kC&pg=PA40|passage=Pliny addeth this '''ſingularity''' to the Indian ſoil, that it is without weeds, that the second year the very falling down of the seeds yieldeth corn.}}
#* {{RQ:Addison Italy|passage=I took notice of this little figure for the '''singularity''' of the instrument.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=16 August 80|volume=8|number=5|journal=Gay Community News|page=10|author=Michael Bronski|title=Does Life Incriminate Art?|passage=A sub-cultural style or artifact, when adopted by the mainstream, loses its '''singularity'''. Once bell-bottoms became fashionable they were no longer a "gay style."}}
# An unusual [[action]] or [[behaviour]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=a. 1790|author=w:Benjamin Franklin|title=Autobiography of Benjamin Franklin|chapter=Beginning Life as a Printer|location=New York|publisher=Henry Holt and Company|year_published=1916|url=https://www.gutenberg.org/files/20203/20203-h/20203-h.htm|passage=When about 16 years of age I happened to meet with a book, written by one Tryon, recommending a vegetable diet. I determined to go into it. {{...}} My refusing to eat flesh occasioned an inconveniency, and I was frequently chid for my '''singularity'''.}}
#* {{RQ:Landon Francesca Carrara|volume=II|page=115|passage="Do you know," said she to Guido one morning, when, after asking her to sing, the Englishman had left the room in the very middle of her song, "that I have taken a fancy into my head, which quite accounts for Mr. Arden's '''singularities''': it is, that I am like some one whom he loved and lost in early youth; and though the loss is dreadful, the love is yet pleasant to remember."}}
# A point where all [[parallel]] [[line]]s [[meet]].
# A point where a measured variable reaches unmeasurable or infinite value.
# {{lb|en|mathematics}} The [[value]] or [[range]] of values of a [[function]] for which a [[derivative]] does not [[exist]].
# {{lb|en|physics}} {{ellipsis of|en|gravitational singularity|t=a [[point]] or [[region]] in [[spacetime]] in which [[gravitational]] [[force]]s cause [[matter]] to have an [[infinite]] [[density]]; associated with [[black hole]]s}}.
#: {{syn|en|spacetime singularity}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1988|author=w:Stephen Hawking|title=w:A Brief History of Time|publisher=w:Bantam Books|page=88|pageurl=https://archive.org/details/briefhistoryofti0000hawk/page/88/mode/1up|isbn=978-1-4090-9236-0|passage=At this '''singularity''' the laws of science and our ability to predict the future would break down. However, any observer who remained outside the black hole would not be affected by this failure of predictability, because neither light nor any other signal could reach him from the '''singularity'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1992|author=Jean-Pierre Luminet|title=Black Holes|publisher=w:Cambridge University Press|page=135|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=WRexJODPq5AC&pg=PA135|isbn=978-0-521-40906-3|passage=Consequently the interior of a black hole is empty, with a '''singularity''' at the centre.}}
# {{lb|en|sometimes|capitalized}} {{ellipsis of|en|technological singularity|t=a hypothetical turning point in the future, the culmination of ever-accelerating technological progress, when human history as we have known it ends, and a strange new era begins. For some writers, the catalyst is superhuman machine intelligence}}.
#: {{syn|en|technological singularity|Kurzweil singularity|Singularity}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Stan Ulam|year=1958|title=Tribute to John von Neumann|work=Bulletin of the American Mathematical Society|passage=One conversation centered on the ever accelerating progress of technology and changes in the mode of human life, which gives the appearance of approaching some essential singularity in the history of the race beyond which human affairs, as we know them, could not continue.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Vernor Vinge|title=The Coming Technological Singularity: How to Survive in the Post-Human Era|year=1993|work=Whole Earth Review|passage=Within thirty years, we will have the technological means to create superhuman intelligence. Shortly after, the human era will be ended. {{...}} I think it's fair to call this event a singularity ("the Singularity" for the purposes of this paper).}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|date=2011-01-05|author=Rob Walker|title=Cyberspace when you're dead|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/magazine/09Immortality-t.html|passage={{quote-gloss|Vernor}} Vinge was among those (along with, notably, Ray Kurzweil) to discuss the transformation of humans by technology, coming in a matter of decades, referred to as "the '''singularity'''."|brackets=on|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180303044713/https://www.nytimes.com/2011/01/09/magazine/09Immortality-t.html}}
#* {{RQ:NYT|date=2016-04-07|author=John Markoff|title=When is the '''Singularity'''? Probably not in your lifetime|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2016/04/07/science/artificial-intelligence-when-is-the-singularity.html|passage=The notion of the '''Singularity''' is predicated on Moore's Law, the 1965 observation by the Intel co-founder Gordon Moore, that the number of transistors that can be etched onto a sliver of silicon doubles at roughly two year intervals.|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20180402084445/https://www.nytimes.com/2016/04/07/science/artificial-intelligence-when-is-the-singularity.html}}
#* {{quote-av|en|title=I/O 2026 Keynote|at=5:40|date=2026-05-22|url=https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=34q_KjF64E8|author=w:Demis Hassabis|passage=When we look back at this time, I think we all realize that we were standing in the foothills of the '''singularity'''.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Anything [[singular]], rare, or curious.
#* {{RQ:Shakespeare Winter's Tale|act=V|scene=iii|page=302|passage=Your Gallerie / Haue vve paſs'd through, not vvithout much content / In many '''ſingularities'''; {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Markham English Husbandman|book=Former|chapter=I|chaptername=The Proem of the Author. What a Husbandman is: His Vtilitie and Necessitie|sig=A2|page=6|passage=[N]either my Birth, my Education, nor the generall courſe of my life can promiſe no '''ſingularitie''' in any part of thoſe Actes they treate of: {{...}}}}
#* {{RQ:Lindsay Age of Consent|year=1962|page=89|passage=He was badgered in that witness-box for an hour. By a distracting repetition of cross-examination he was forced to confess that he had seen and spoken to a human biped in broad daylight, yet could not recollect one '''singularity''' to distinguish this phantom from the flat mass of humanity.}}
# {{lb|en|obsolete}} Possession of a particular or exclusive privilege, prerogative, or distinction.
#* {{RQ:Hooker Laws|passage=St. Gregory, being himself a Bishop of Rome, and writing against the title of ''Universal Bishop'', saith thus, "None of all my predecessors ever consented to use this ungodly title; no bishop of Rome ever took upon him this name of '''singularity'''."}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1659|author=[[w:John Pearson (bishop)|John Pearson]]|title=An Exposition of the Creed|page=445|pageurl=https://books.google.com/books?id=cRY2AQAAMAAJ&pg=PA445|passage=Catholicism {{...}} must be understood in opposition to the legal '''singularity''' of the Jewish nation.}}
# [[celibacy|Celibacy]], [[singleness]] (as contrasted with marriage).
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1655|title=Eniautos: A Covrse of Sermons for All the Sundays of the Year|author=Jeremy Taylor|page=223|passage=Marriage is the mother of the world, and preserves Kingdomes, and fils Cities, and Churches, and Heaven itself: Celibate, like the flie in the heart of an apple, dwels in a perpetuall sweetnesse, but sits alone, and is confin'd, and dies in '''singularity'''; but marriage, like the useful bee, builds a house and gathers sweetnesse from every flower, and labours and unites into Societies and Republicks, and sends out colonies, and feeds the world with delicacies, and obeys its king, and keeps order, and exercises many vertues, and promotes the interest of mankind, and is that state of good things to which God hath designed the present constitution of the world.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1995|title=Arguing About Sex: The Rhetoric of Christian Sexual Morality|author=Joseph Monti|page=234|passage=Gradually the implication of biblical monotheism created an entailment of '''singularity''' and monogamy in sexual relations.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1998|author=Judith A. Merkle|chapter=Two Faces of Sexual Integration|title=A Different Touch: A Study of Vows in Religious Life|publisher=Liturgical Press|isbn=9780814624654|page=248|passage=Comparisons between marriage and celibacy are dubious. {{...}} In this sense, marriage is the institution of sexual partnering whereas celibacy is an institution of sexual '''singularity'''.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2015|author=Susan J. Pollard|title=Celibacy and Soul: Exploring the Depths of Chastity|publisher=Fisher King Press|isbn=9781771690133|page=59|passage=David emphasized that being singular in his relationship with God relies on real ties to the community, real friendships and a real work that sustains him. As I write, I am conscious of a '''singularity''' that I live and that is supported by close friends, family, clients and religious community. Genuine relationships are crucial and provide a supportive structure of interdependence.}}

