===Etymology===
{{etymon|en|:bor|nl:snappen|nds:snappen|tree=1}}
From {{bor|en|nl|snappen|t=to bite; seize}} or {{bor|en|nds|snappen|t=to bite; seize}}, ultimately from {{der|en|gmw-pro|*snappōn}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*snappōną|t=to snap; snatch; chatter}}, intensive form of {{m|gem-pro|*snapāną}} {{gloss|”to snap; grab”, whence {{cog|non|snapa|t=to get; scrounge}}}}, from {{der|en|ine-pro|*snep-|*snop-}}; compare {{cog|lt|snãpas||beak, bill}}.<ref>{{R:gem:Kroonen:2011|249}}</ref> (One alternative hypothesis links the Germanic words to {{m|gem-pro||*snu-}}, an {{sound-symbolic|en|title=expressive}} root deriving words meaning “nose”, “{{l|en|snout}}”, “{{l|en|sniff}}” etc.,<ref name="EO"/> but this is phonetically unsound.)  In any case influenced by {{onom|en|title=onomatopoeia}}; note expressions such as {{m|en|snip-snap}}, containing the formally unrelated {{m|en|snip}}.

Cognate with {{cog|fy|snappe||to get; catch; snap}}, {{cog|de|schnappen||to grab}}, {{cog|sv|snappa||to snatch}}.

The verb is derived from the noun.<ref name="EO">{{R:Etymonline}}</ref>

